// A container php::print_r cannot render must be refused BY ITS OWN NAME, and
// must say WHICH piece is missing.
//
// This used to descend four levels into libstdc++'s red-black tree and refuse
// there:
//
//   php::print_r: member '_M_t': member '_M_impl': member '_M_header':
//   member '_M_color': no dumper for type '_Rb_tree_color' yet
//
// — naming a type the user never wrote and never once saying std::map. A
// container's members are the library's internals, not the user's data, so a
// container is refused (or walked) AT ITSELF, before any descent.
//
// std::map, std::set and std::list are no longer the subject: they RENDER now,
// through the generated iterator loop (tests/testphpdumpiter.mad). What remains
// refused is a genuine and PERMANENT limit rather than an arbitrary one, and the
// message states it:
//
// the generated loop is COUNTED off size(), because libstdc++ declares
// operator== / operator!= on every standard iterator as FRIEND FREE functions
// (stl_tree.h 315/320, stl_list.h 318/324) — findMethod cannot see a free
// function, so `it != c.end()` cannot be generated at all. A container with
// begin()/end() and NO size() therefore has no bound, and saying so is the only
// honest answer. It costs nothing for the containers that DO render: var_dump's
// head line states the count anyway.
//
// The gate is the MESSAGE. A refusal that goes back to naming _Rb_tree_color, or
// that stops naming the missing piece, no longer matches this fixture.
struct It {
	int *p;
	int &operator*() { return *p; }
	It &operator++() { p = p + 1; return *this; }
};

struct NoSize {
	int d[3];
	It begin() { It i; i.p = d; return i; }
	It end() { It i; i.p = d + 3; return i; }
};

int main()
{
	NoSize n;
	n.d[0] = 1;
	php::print_r(n);
	return 0;
}
